      "text": "The ball rises as $243 \\times \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^n$ cm in the $n$th bounce.\nWe want to find the smallest $n$ such that $243 \\times \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^n < 30.$\nTo solve this inequality, we can isolate the exponent on one side and take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n$\\ln\\left(243 \\times \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^n\\right) < \\ln 30.$\nUsing the properties of logarithms, we can expand both sides:\n$\\ln 243 + n \\ln \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) < \\ln 30.$\nSimplifying further, we get:\n$n \\ln \\frac{2}{3} < \\ln \\frac{30}{243}.$\nDividing both sides by $\\ln \\frac{2}{3},$ we find:\n$n > \\frac{\\ln \\frac{30}{243}}{\\ln \\frac{2}{3}}.$\nPlugging in the values, we have:\n$n > \\frac{\\ln \\frac{30}{243}}{\\ln \\frac{2}{3}} \\approx \\boxed{3}.$\nTherefore, the ball first rises less than $30$ cm after the $3$rd bounce.The answer is: 3",
